y issue may be mitigated by checklists. However, the nasty remarks are serious. But C also has egocentric attitude.\n\nWe need to make a focused argument, referencing discussion. Perhaps we can say we still think B is best because his reliability and stress resilience are proven, and his technical competence is strong, while C's egocentric attitude and unwillingness to further education raise serious concerns about adaptability, which is critical. Also C's communication difficulty may affect crew. So we keep vote B.\n\nWe must include explicit facts: B very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills. C egocentric, not willing to further education, difficulty communicating ideas. Also note that we have no evidence on B's adaptability, but we can argue that his conscientiousness suggests ability to follow procedures and accept feedback? But we cannot infer. We can note lack of evidence but still weigh other strengths.\n\nThus we will keep vote B, stating why.\n\nPublic message: reference Agent 3's point about lack of B adaptability evidence, but argue that B's proven reliability and stress resilience are more critical, and C's egocentric and unwillingness to learn are more concerning for adaptability. Also note that C's communication difficulty may be as problematic as B's nasty remarks. So we maintain vote B.\n\nLet's produce."}
